# 6. Core Counseling Attributes (NCE content outline, 8%): 16 items
CAT = "nceAttrib"; SEG = "ATT"
Q = [
("On Carkhuff's five-level scale, an empathic response that captures the client's feeling and meaning without adding or losing anything is level:",
 "3", ["1", "2", "5"],
 "Level 3 is the interchangeable response, the minimum facilitative level. Levels 1 and 2 subtract from what the client said, and levels 4 and 5 add deeper meaning."),
("A counselor accepts a client fully as a person while disagreeing with his choices. This reflects:",
 "Unconditional positive regard", ["Conditional acceptance", "Countertransference", "Defensive professional neutrality"],
 "Unconditional positive regard is warm acceptance of the client as a person without conditions of worth, which is not the same as approving of every behavior."),
("Accurate empathy is best described as:",
 "Sensing the client's world as if yours", ["Feeling sorry for the client", "Sharing a similar life experience of your own", "Agreeing with what the client says"],
 "Rogers described empathy as sensing the client's private world as if it were your own, without losing the as if quality. Sympathy is feeling sorry for someone."),
("A client says, \"I'm so tired of trying and getting nowhere.\" Which response shows the most empathy?",
 "\"You feel worn out and discouraged.\"", ["\"Everyone feels that way sometimes.\"", "\"Have you tried a new approach?\"", "\"You shouldn't give up so easily.\""],
 "An empathic response reflects the client's feeling and meaning. Reassurance, advice and judgment move away from the client's experience."),
("A counselor feels irritated by a client who reminds her of her critical father. This is an example of:",
 "Countertransference", ["Transference", "Projection by the client", "Resistance"],
 "Countertransference is the counselor's emotional reaction to a client shaped by the counselor's own history. Transference is the client's reaction to the counselor."),
("What should a counselor do when she notices strong countertransference toward a client?",
 "Seek supervision and reflect", ["Tell the client about her father", "Ignore it and carry on", "End counseling right away"],
 "Self-awareness and supervision help the counselor understand the reaction and keep it from affecting the work. Disclosing it to the client is rarely helpful."),
("A counselor openly invites a client to talk about how race may affect their counseling relationship. This is called:",
 "Broaching", ["Blocking", "Linking", "Reframing"],
 "Broaching means intentionally raising race, ethnicity and culture as factors in the client's concerns or the counseling relationship."),
("The Multicultural and Social Justice Counseling Competencies begin with which quadrant?",
 "Counselor self-awareness", ["Client advocacy", "Research methods", "Diagnostic accuracy and coding"],
 "The competencies move from counselor self-awareness to client worldview, the counseling relationship and counseling and advocacy interventions."),
("A counselor says she is colorblind and treats every client exactly the same. Why can this be a problem?",
 "It ignores how culture shapes clients", ["It is the ideal multicultural stance", "It builds rapport faster", "It is required by ethics codes"],
 "A colorblind stance overlooks how race and culture shape clients' experiences, including discrimination, and can leave clients feeling unseen."),
("Cultural humility in counseling means:",
 "Ongoing self-reflection and openness", ["Mastering facts about every culture", "Avoiding cultural topics in session", "Matching every client by race"],
 "Cultural humility is a lifelong stance of self-examination, openness to the client's expertise on their own culture and attention to power differences."),
("A client describes beliefs the counselor strongly disagrees with. A nonjudgmental stance means the counselor:",
 "Sets aside her values to understand", ["Explains why the client is wrong", "Refers the client elsewhere at once", "Changes the subject quickly"],
 "A nonjudgmental stance means suspending evaluation of the client to understand their view. Counselors avoid imposing their own values (ACA standard A.4.b)."),
("A client angrily criticizes the counselor's approach. The counselor stays calm and explores the concern. This shows:",
 "Conflict tolerance", ["Defensive withdrawal", "Passive compliance", "Counter-attack"],
 "Tolerating conflict without becoming defensive lets the counselor repair ruptures and model healthy ways to handle disagreement."),
("Which nonverbal behavior conveys attending in many Western settings?",
 "Leaning slightly toward the client", ["Crossing arms tightly", "Checking the clock often", "Turning partly away from the client"],
 "Egan's SOLER cues, such as facing the client squarely, an open posture and leaning slightly forward, signal attention. Their meaning can differ across cultures."),
("A client uses they/them pronouns. What shows sensitivity to gender identity?",
 "Using the client's pronouns", ["Using pronouns from the intake form", "Avoiding pronouns altogether", "Asking the family which to use"],
 "Respecting a client's name and pronouns communicates acceptance and safety. Mistakes are corrected briefly and without fuss."),
("A counselor notices she feels drained after sessions with trauma survivors. Self-awareness suggests she should:",
 "Monitor her limits and use self-care", ["Take on more trauma clients", "Hide it from her supervisor", "Stop asking any clients about trauma"],
 "Counselors who notice the impact of clients' material on themselves watch their limits, use supervision and practice self-care to stay effective."),
("A group leader helps members feel they are not alone because others share their struggles. This fosters:",
 "Universality", ["Catharsis", "Altruism", "Cohesiveness"],
 "Universality, one of Yalom's therapeutic factors, is the relief of learning that others have similar problems."),
]
